#X obj 652 11 array.window;
#X obj 230 78 cnv 1 535 463 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 83 The object fills the specified array with the selected
window function. The window type can be set with the @type property
\, by one of the corresponding methods (hann \, hamming \, blackman
\, etc.) \, or by sending a bang after setting the type. When the array
is filled \, a bang is sent out through the outlet.;
#X text 240 184 Window functions in brief: 'rect' — rectangular window
\, all samples equal to 1 \, minimal main-lobe width but high side
lobes (use for transient or when the signal already fits the array
length). 'tri' — triangular window \, simple linear taper to zero at
both ends. 'welch' — parabolic taper \, similar to tri but smoother.
'sine' — sine window \, smooth taper \, good general-purpose choice.
'hann' — Hann window \, raised cosine \, one of the most common choices
for spectral analysis and FFT processing. 'hamming' — Hamming window
\, similar to Hann but with a small non-zero offset at the edges \,
slightly lower first side lobe. 'blackman' — Blackman window \, lower
side lobes than Hann/Hamming \, at the cost of a wider main lobe (good
for accurate amplitude measurements). 'blackman-harris' — even lower
side lobes \, excellent for high-dynamic-range spectral analysis. 'nuttall'
— Nuttall window \, similar to Blackman-Harris with very low side lobes.
'flattop' — flat-top window \, very wide main lobe but extremely flat
top \, best for accurate amplitude measurements of specific spectral
peaks. 'gauss' — Gaussian window \, smooth \, adjustable via the standard
deviation \, often used for time-frequency analysis (STFT \, spectrograms).;
